---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Areas of Interest" description="Domains of practice — streaming, lakehouse, platform design, observability — without project claims.">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">Areas of Interest</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-[var(--color-muted)]">
    Areas of practice, not a project list. The writing here keeps orbiting
    these four.
  </p>

  <section id="streaming" class="prose prose-engineering mt-10 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Streaming Systems</h2>
    <p>
      Event-driven architectures, partitioned logs, and stateful stream
      processing. The specific things I keep coming back to: state backends
      and the cost of checkpointing, the trade-off between in-broker compute
      (Kafka Streams, ksqlDB) and dedicated processors like Flink,
      exactly-once delivery as it actually behaves in production, and the
      slow merge between streaming engines and analytical ones around
      materialized views over open table formats.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lakehouse" class="prose prose-engineering mt-10 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Lakehouse Architectures</h2>
    <p>
      Open table formats (Iceberg, Delta, Hudi), the REST catalog spec, and
      the slow standardization of metadata across query engines. I'm
      particularly drawn to the operational side: metadata evolution,
      partition layout, file sizing, compaction strategy at scale. The parts
      of a lakehouse that don't fit on a marketing slide and that you only
      really learn about when something starts costing too much or running
      too slow.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="platform" class="prose prose-engineering mt-10 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Data Platform Design</h2>
    <p>
      The platform layer between raw data and the analytical or operational
      consumers on top of it: ingestion, transformation, orchestration, the
      semantic layer, access control, discovery. What I find interesting is
      the contracts a platform offers and, more importantly, the ones it
      refuses to. How to grow ownership outward without fragmenting the
      stack. How to evolve a central platform toward decentralized data
      product ownership without cargo-culting data mesh in the process.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="observability" class="prose prose-engineering mt-10 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Observability &amp; Reliability</h2>
    <p>
      Data observability in the conventional sense (freshness, volume, schema
      drift, distribution shifts, lineage) and the larger question underneath:
      what does reliability even mean when the failure mode is "wrong numbers"
      instead of "service down"? OpenLineage and similar standards interest
      me. So do the limits of contract testing, and how teams actually make
      incident response feel real for data systems instead of theatre.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
